      <section class="topic-section" id="similar-trianlges">
        <h2>Similar Triangles</h2>

	<p>
	  The idea of trigonometry in euclidean geometry stems from the concept of <span class = "emph">similar triangles</span>. As a reminder,
          two triangles sharing two identical angles are said to be similar:
	</p>

	<MathCanvas2D
	  id="similar-triangles"
	  label="An illustration of the relationship between two similar triangles."
	  aspectRatio="16 / 9"
	/>

	<p>
	  Of course, even from the above illustration it is evident that fixing two angles actually fixes all three of them, since we know the angles inside a triangle
	always sum up to <Math tex="180^{\circ}"/>:
	</p>

	<MathCanvas2D
	  id="angle-sum"
	  label="An illustration of how the sum of angles within a triangle is constant."
	  aspectRatio="16 / 9"
	/>

	<p>
	  So given the three angles of a triangle, <Math tex="\alpha, \beta, \gamma"/>, fixing <Math tex="\alpha"/> and <Math tex="\beta"/> automatically fixes <Math tex="\gamma"/> too:
	</p>

	<Equation
	  tex={String.raw`{\red \alpha} + {\red \beta} + \gamma = 180^{\circ} \Rightarrow \gamma = 180^{\circ} - { \red \alpha } - { \red \beta }.`}
	/>

	<p>
	  Going back to the similar triangles example, if you look at corresponding pairs of same colored angles
        from each triangle you'll notice the ratio of sides opposite to those angles
        is always the same. Say both triangles have the same two angles <Math tex="\alpha"/> and <Math tex="\beta"/>. In the smaller triangle, sides sitting
	opposite to <Math tex="\alpha"/> and <Math tex="\beta"/> are <Math tex="\text{side}_1"/> and <Math tex="\text{side}_2"/> respectively, and in the bigger one they
	are <Math tex="\text{Side}_1"/> and <Math tex="\text{Side}_2"/>. Then we can write down the relationship:
	</p>

	<Equation
	  tex={String.raw`
	  \frac{ \text{side}_1 }{ \text{Side}_1 } = \frac{ \text{side}_2 }{ \text{Side}_2 }.
          `}
	/>

	<p>
	  More important here though is not the relationship between the two triangles, but the relationship that the sides of one triangle have between each other. Rearranging:
	</p>

	<Equation
	  tex={String.raw`
	  \frac{ \text{side}_1 }{ \text{Side}_1 } = \frac{ \text{side}_2 }{ \text{Side}_2 } \iff \frac{ \text{side}_1 }{ \text{side}_2 } = \frac{ \text{Side}_1 }{ \text{Side}_2 }.
          `}
	/>

	<p>
	  In a way, fixing the angles of triangles makes them, you could say, ratio invariant. That means if we know what the angles of a triangle are
        (namely, it is sufficient to only know two of them), while we cannot determine the <i>lengths</i> of its sides based on this information alone, we can always
	know what the <i>ratios</i> between any two sides will be.
	</p>

	<p>
	  Now, we could technically develop some sort of general theory that assigns any two
          angles <Math tex="\alpha"/> and <Math tex="\beta"/> (or at least supposing they are less than <Math tex="180^{\circ}"/>) to a series of associated ratios
          (there are <Math tex="A_3^2 = 6"/> different ratios we could compute between sides of a triangle). But trigonometry mainly deals with a particular class of triangles:
	  <span class="emph"> right triangles</span>. Right triangles are the canonical choice because they make the math easier and, as we'll see later down the line, the
	  theory we develop around right triangles can be nonetheless applied to any regular ol' triangle as well, albeit in a slightly modified form.
	</p>
	
      </section>

      <section class="topic-section" id="right-triangles">
        <h2>Right Triangles</h2>

	<p>
	  That brings to our main subject of study (for now): right triangles (and by <i>that</i> I mean the previous section - which you may or may have not bothered to read ig).
	  That means we'll permanently lock in one of the angles: there will necessarily be a <Math tex="90^{\circ}"/> angle in all triangles we consider, as it can be seen
	  in the two similar triangles below that totally and positively DO NOT violate <span class="emph">Pythagoras's theorem</span>:
	</p>

	<MathCanvas2D
	  id="similar-right-triangles"
	  label="An illustration of the relationship between two similar right triangles."
	  aspectRatio="16 / 9"
	/>

	<p>
	  Alright, all triangles you could obtain by interacting with the picture do in fact violate <span class="emph">Pythagoras's theorem</span>, but this is just for illustrative
	purposes and nice whole lengths are more visually appealing. Don't lynch me.
	</p>

	<p>
	  Since we already know one of the angle is <Math tex="90^{\circ}"/>, by fixing another angle, say <Math tex="\theta"/>, and by applying the previoulsy discussed reasoning,
          we can know for sure what the ratio between any two sides is going to be - no matter their lengths - so
	  we can establish a <span class="emph"> one-to-one </span> correspondence (or at least for the time being one-to-one) between a given angle <Math tex="\theta"/> and a
          real value representing a certain ratio. A right triangle has three sides (indeed, a truly remarkable property that right triangles possess):
          two shorter sides - the legs, and the biggest side (opposite to the right angle) - the hypotenuse. So depending on what ratio we're interested in, we could
          associate <Math tex="\theta"/> with one of the ratios:
	</p>

	<Equation
	  tex={String.raw`
	  \frac{ \text{opposite leg} }{ \text{hypotenuse} },
	  \frac{ \text{hypotenuse} }{ \text{opposite leg} },
          \frac{ \text{adjacent leg} }{ \text{hypotenuse} },
	  \frac{ \text{hypotenuse} }{ \text{adjacent leg} },
	  \frac{ \text{opposite leg} }{ \text{adjacent leg} },
	  \frac{ \text{adjacent leg} }{ \text{opposite leg} }.
          `}
	/>

	<p> We are going to assign names to each of these: </p>

	<Equation
	  tex={String.raw`
	  \sin\theta = \frac{ \text{opposite leg} }{ \text{hypotenuse} } \text{ (sine function)},
          `}
	/>

	<Equation
	  tex={String.raw`
          \cos\theta = \frac{ \text{adjacent leg} }{ \text{hypotenuse} } \text{ (cosine function)},
          `}
	/>

	<Equation
	  tex={String.raw`
	  \tan\theta = \frac{ \text{opposite leg} }{ \text{adjacent leg} } \text{ (tangent function)},
          `}
	/>

	<Equation
	  tex={String.raw`
	  \cot\theta = \frac{ \text{adjacent leg} }{ \text{opposite leg} } \text{ (cotangent function)},
          `}
	/>

	<Equation
	  tex={String.raw`
	  \sec\theta = \frac{ \text{hypotenuse} }{ \text{adjacent leg} } \text{ (secant function)},
          `}
	/>

	<Equation
	  tex={String.raw`
	  \csc\theta = \frac{ \text{hypotenuse} }{ \text{opposite leg} } \text{ (cosecant function)}.
          `}
	/>

	<p>
	  These are known as the <span class="emph">trigonometric functions</span><HoverNote>
	    We haven't exactly defined them as functions yet, but we are going to in the next section, where we'll significantly extend their domain; for now they are restrained
            to angles under <Math tex="90^{\circ}" />. </HoverNote>. Their names come from Latin, or mistranslated Arabic, or both (we aren't exactly historians around here).
            Sine refers to a <i>chord</i> or some sort of <i>curvature</i>, tangent means
	<i> touch</i> and secant has to do with <i>cutting through</i> something - these we'll make a lot more sense when we introduce circles to the study of trigonometry.
	You'll probably notice the other three functions are basically seen as complementary - whatever that means - to the aforementioned ones.
	</p>

	<p>
	  Up until this point, there have been plenty of claims that knowing the angles lets us know the ratios between sides as well. But we haven't clarified how we're supposed
	  to extract that information exactly. What's sine of <Math tex="11^{\circ}"/>? Or tangent of <Math tex="72^{\circ}"/>? What about secant of <Math tex="36^{\circ}"/>?
          If you think about how we defined trigonometric
	  functions, the question of what values do they take turns into infinitely many geometry problems, depending on the angle <Math tex="\theta"/>. And for the vast majority
	  of angles out there, it is no easy task to determine these numbers. In fact, we aren't even going to attempt to precisely capture, for example, the sine of EVERY angle -
          that would impractical and, in a sense, impossible<HoverNote>We'll see how to approximate trigonometric functions at least, but that's in a future section.</HoverNote>.
	  However, we'll discuss the <i>three fundamental angles</i> for which the values of these trigonometric functions are widely known and fairly
	  easy to obtain: <Math tex="30^{\circ}"/>, <Math tex="45^{\circ}"/> and <Math tex="60^{\circ}"/>.
	</p>

	<p>
	  For starters, looking at the <Math tex="6"/> ratios, there seem to be some sort of underlying relationships between them - surely we don't have to compute all of them
	  separately. What's the minimum then? It shouldn't take too much digging to observe:
	</p>

	<Equation
	  tex={String.raw`
	  \tan\theta = \frac{ \sin\theta }{ \cos\theta },
          `}
	/>

	<Equation
	  tex={String.raw`
	  \sec\theta = \frac{ 1 }{ \cos\theta },
          `}
	/>

	<Equation
	  tex={String.raw`
	  \csc\theta = \frac{ 1 }{ \sin\theta }.
          `}
	/>

	<p>
	  So all these values mainly depend on sine and cosine
	<HoverNote>This might be less obvious, but if you meditate on it for a bit, knowing the sine tells us what the cosine is as well and vice versa - so we only need to find
	either one of them. More on that later.</HoverNote>. With this knowledge in mind, we set out to compute these two functions in particular, for the values mentioned earlier.
	</p>

	<MathCanvas2D
	  id="sine30-proof"
	  label="An illustration of how the sum of angles within a triangle is constant."
	  aspectRatio="16 / 9"
	/>

	<MathCanvas2D
